q('A patient has subacute headache, cranial neuropathy and hydrocephalus. CSF shows lymphocytic pleocytosis, high protein and low glucose. Which infection is a major concern?', 'Tuberculous meningitis',[
 ('Uncomplicated enteroviral meningitis as the characteristic explanation','Markedly low glucose, cranial neuropathy and hydrocephalus raise other concerns.'),('PML as the typical meningitic process','PML is primarily a demyelinating white-matter disease.'),('Latent TB infection without active disease','Latent infection should not cause this CNS syndrome.')],
 'Basal meningeal inflammation can cause obstructive and vascular complications', '**TB meningitis can produce cranial neuropathies, infarction and hydrocephalus.**',['CSF findings support but do not uniquely establish the diagnosis.','Cryptococcal and other chronic meningitides remain alternatives.'], '**Obtain appropriate CSF molecular and culture studies when safe.**',['Negative NAAT does not exclude all TB meningitis.','Urgent treatment of high-probability disease must not await culture growth.'],[['Course','Subacute'],['CSF','Often high protein and low glucose'],['Complications','Cranial neuropathy, infarction and hydrocephalus']],[EX,AD],style='clinical_scenario')
q('In an HIV-negative adult with tuberculous meningitis, which adjunct is generally recommended with anti-TB treatment?', 'A tapering corticosteroid course such as dexamethasone or prednisolone',[
 ('Corticosteroids instead of anti-TB drugs','They do not eradicate the organism.'),('Routine withholding of all steroids because the cause is infectious','TB meningitis is a specific indication for adjunctive treatment.'),('A short pulmonary-TB regimen without CNS-specific planning','CNS disease requires site-appropriate treatment.')],
 'Inflammatory injury contributes to neurological harm in TB meningitis', '**Adjunctive corticosteroids improve outcomes in the appropriate evidence-based population.**',['They accompany, rather than replace, effective multidrug therapy.','HIV-associated disease needs its own evidence and timing assessment.'], '**Monitor neurological complications and treatment toxicity.**',['Hydrocephalus may require neurosurgical management.','Assess glucose, secondary infection and other steroid effects.'],[['Core','Effective CNS TB regimen'],['Adjunct','Tapering corticosteroids'],['Complications','Pressure, hydrocephalus and vascular injury']],[WT,OT])
q('According to WHO guidance, when should ART generally begin in drug-susceptible nonmeningeal TB/HIV coinfection?', 'As soon as possible within two weeks of starting TB treatment, regardless of CD4 count',[
 ('After all TB treatment is completed','This delays the benefits of HIV treatment.'),('Only after CD4 rises spontaneously above 200','ART is needed to restore immune function.'),('The same immediate timing must be applied without exception to TB meningitis','Meningitis is a specific exception.')],
 'Early ART reduces the consequences of ongoing immunodeficiency', '**WHO favors initiation within two weeks in the specified nonmeningeal setting.**',['Different national and specialty guidelines can express timing by CD4 stratum.','Drug-resistant disease and CNS involvement require specific guidance.'], '**Check rifamycin interactions before selecting ART.**',['Use an effective compatible regimen.','Monitor for IRIS while continuing necessary infection treatment.'],[['Scope','WHO nonmeningeal drug-susceptible TB'],['Timing','Within two weeks'],['Planning','Interactions and IRIS monitoring']],[WT])
q('Why is ART timing different in HIV-associated TB meningitis from routine nonmeningeal TB?', 'Early intracranial inflammatory worsening and serious adverse events can outweigh the benefit of immediate initiation',[
 ('ART has no eventual role in TB meningitis','It remains essential after appropriate timing assessment.'),('TB meningitis permanently cures HIV replication','It does not.'),('Rifampicin prevents every possible ART regimen from being used','Interactions can be managed with suitable regimen selection.')],
 'The confined CNS space changes the consequence of immune inflammation', '**TB meningitis requires coordinated specialist timing.**',['Do not automatically apply the routine immediate-start pathway.','Guidelines differ in the precise interval and clinical prerequisites.'], '**Begin effective TB treatment and manage neurological disease first.**',['Assess control of CNS infection and drug toxicity.','Plan and document ART initiation rather than deferring it indefinitely.'],[['Nonmeningeal TB','Early ART usually favored'],['TB meningitis','Specific delayed/tailored pathway'],['Goal','Safe immune restoration after CNS assessment']],[HI,WT])
q('A patient has a lymphocyte-predominant exudative pleural effusion and elevated pleural ADA in a high-TB-prevalence setting. Which interpretation is best?', 'The result supports pleural TB probability, but tissue/culture evidence and competing causes should still be assessed',[
 ('ADA alone proves the organism and full susceptibility profile','It does not provide species or susceptibility information.'),('Every elevated ADA is a pyogenic empyema','Several causes can raise ADA.'),('A negative pleural-fluid smear reliably excludes pleural TB','Pleural TB is often paucibacillary.')],
 'A host-response marker is interpreted through local prevalence', '**ADA can support diagnosis in the appropriate pleural syndrome.**',['Lymphoma and other inflammatory or infectious conditions can overlap.','Pleural biopsy can provide histological and microbiological evidence.'], '**Use a source-appropriate diagnostic plan.**',['Obtain culture material where feasible.','Avoid assuming an empirical label excludes malignancy when the course is atypical.'],[['ADA','Supportive marker'],['Prevalence','Changes predictive value'],['Tissue/culture','Etiology and resistance evidence']],[PL,EX],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with suspected spinal TB develops progressive leg weakness and bladder dysfunction. What is the best next action?', 'Urgent spinal imaging and specialist assessment for cord compression, instability and the need for intervention',[
 ('Wait several months to assess antibiotics alone without imaging','Progressive neurological compromise requires urgent evaluation.'),('Prescribe bed rest as the only treatment','This does not address the infection or compression.'),('Assume neurological symptoms are a benign drug effect without assessing the spine','The clinical localization suggests an urgent structural threat.')],
 'Spinal infection can cause irreversible neurological injury', '**Compression, epidural disease and instability need prompt anatomical assessment.**',['MRI defines neural and soft-tissue involvement.','Tissue sampling can establish diagnosis and susceptibility.'], '**Combine effective TB therapy with an appropriate structural plan.**',['Surgery is selected for neurological deficit, instability or other indications.','Use multidisciplinary spine and infectious-disease care.'],[['Red flag','Weakness and sphincter dysfunction'],['Imaging','Urgent MRI when feasible'],['Treatment','Antimicrobials plus indicated decompression/stabilization']],[EX],style='clinical_scenario')
q('What does a miliary pattern in disseminated TB reflect?', 'Hematogenous spread producing numerous small lesions across the lungs and potentially other organs',[
 ('Disease necessarily confined to a single large bronchus','Miliary spread is disseminated.'),('A radiological pattern unique enough to determine susceptibility without testing','It does not provide resistance information and has mimics.'),('Latent infection without active organism dissemination','Miliary disease is active and potentially severe.')],
 'Dissemination can produce a multisystem illness', '**The pattern can accompany fever, hypoxemia and organ involvement.**',['Advanced HIV can produce atypical imaging.','Other metastatic, fungal and inflammatory patterns enter the differential.'], '**Seek microbiological evidence from suitable sites.**',['Assess CNS and other organ symptoms when relevant.','Treat a severe high-probability illness promptly while confirming the diagnosis.'],[['Mechanism','Bloodstream dissemination'],['Distribution','Lung and extrapulmonary organs'],['Diagnosis','Clinical, imaging and organism evidence']],[EX,AD])
q('An HIV-negative patient with proven susceptible lymph-node TB initially improves, then develops node enlargement during adherent treatment. What is the best principle?', 'Consider a paradoxical inflammatory response after assessing adherence, resistance, alternative disease and complications',[
 ('Every enlarged node proves acquired drug resistance','Paradoxical worsening can occur despite effective therapy.'),('Stop all TB treatment automatically','Effective treatment usually needs to continue while the cause is assessed.'),('Ignore new symptoms because paradoxical reactions are always harmless','Complications and alternative causes require evaluation.')],
 'Inflammatory worsening is not restricted to ART-associated IRIS', '**Paradoxical reactions can occur in people without HIV.**',['The timing and prior response can support the possibility.','It remains a diagnosis requiring exclusion of treatment failure and other causes.'], '**Tailor intervention to severity and anatomical risk.**',['Aspiration, imaging or selected anti-inflammatory therapy may be needed.','Do not escalate drugs blindly without reviewing the evidence.'],[['Prior improvement','Supports paradoxical possibility'],['Exclude','Failure, resistance and alternative diagnosis'],['Response','Severity- and site-specific management']],[OT,EX],style='clinical_scenario')
q('What must be addressed before starting TB preventive treatment in an exposed or infection-test-positive person?', 'Evaluate for and exclude active TB disease through the appropriate clinical pathway',[
 ('A positive immune test automatically proves there is no active disease','It cannot distinguish latent from active disease.'),('Preventive monotherapy is appropriate for every symptomatic active case','This is inadequate treatment and can select resistance.'),('No symptom or imaging assessment is ever needed','The evaluation depends on risk and the recommended pathway.')],
 'Preventive treatment and disease treatment have different purposes', '**TPT reduces progression risk when active disease is absent.**',['Symptoms and relevant imaging or microbiology can be needed before treatment.','The source case’s susceptibility can change the preventive regimen.'], '**Select a regimen compatible with the person’s medicines and circumstances.**',['Check rifamycin interactions.','Counsel about adherence and hepatic warning symptoms.'],[['Active TB','Full multidrug treatment'],['TB infection','Preventive treatment when eligible'],['Before TPT','Disease exclusion and interaction review']],[PT,LP])
q('Which pair describes accepted short-course rifamycin-based TB preventive options in suitable patients?', 'Three months of weekly isoniazid/rifapentine or four months of daily rifampicin',[
 ('Two weeks of ethambutol alone or one week of pyrazinamide','These are not accepted TPT courses.'),('A full BPaLM regimen for every drug-susceptible latent infection','That is not routine susceptible-infection prevention.'),('One dose of rifampicin as universal TB preventive treatment','That does not constitute the standard TB prevention regimens.')],
 'Shorter validated regimens can improve completion', '**3HP and 4R are established options where suitable.**',['Age, pregnancy, HIV treatment and resistance exposure affect eligibility.','Longer isoniazid-based options remain useful in selected circumstances.'], '**Use a regimen-specific schedule and safety plan.**',['Weekly and daily doses must not be confused.','Review interactions and follow up for toxicity.'],[['3HP','Weekly H plus rifapentine for three months'],['4R','Daily rifampicin for four months'],['Selection','Patient, interaction and source-susceptibility context']],[LP,PT])
q('What important MDR/RR-TB contact-prevention option was added as a strong WHO recommendation in 2024?', 'Six months of daily levofloxacin for eligible contacts after active disease exclusion and source-resistance assessment',[
 ('Routine isoniazid alone regardless of the source strain’s resistance','The preventive drug must be appropriate to the exposure pattern.'),('BPaLM for every asymptomatic household contact','A full disease regimen is not routine contact prevention.'),('No preventive treatment can ever be used after MDR/RR exposure','Updated evidence supports an option in eligible contacts.')],
 'Resistance in the source case changes prevention as well as treatment', '**WHO recommends six-month levofloxacin TPT in the appropriate MDR/RR contact pathway.**',['Assess source susceptibility, including fluoroquinolone resistance.','Exclude active disease before preventive treatment.'], '**Provide follow-up and adverse-effect counseling.**',['Age, comorbidity and exposure intensity influence implementation.','Do not apply the regimen mechanically when the source is fluoroquinolone resistant.'],[['Exposure','MDR/RR-TB'],['Option','Six months daily levofloxacin'],['Prerequisites','Disease exclusion and resistance-informed selection']],[PT])
q('Which infection-control approach is appropriate for suspected infectious pulmonary TB in a health-care setting?', 'Airborne precautions with suitable isolation/ventilation and a fit-tested N95 or higher respirator for staff',[
 ('Droplet precautions alone with no airborne strategy','TB is transmitted through airborne particles.'),('Gloves alone as sufficient respiratory protection','They do not prevent inhalation.'),('Wait for culture confirmation before any precautions','Suspected infectious disease warrants prompt control measures.')],
 'Transmission prevention begins before final confirmation', '**Administrative, environmental and respiratory controls work together.**',['Prompt identification and effective treatment reduce exposure.','An airborne infection isolation room is preferred when available.'], '**Use appropriate source control during transport.**',['Mask the patient as indicated and minimize unnecessary movement.','Adapt safe interim arrangements when engineering resources are limited.'],[['Administrative','Prompt recognition and pathway'],['Environmental','Isolation and ventilation'],['Staff protection','Fit-tested N95 or higher']],[AI])

